## What changed in our understanding

The [original incident page](/kb/incidents/4471) says one issue and one queued fix.
Its executable table disagrees: the reports split between a shared parser defect
and a booking-form hint defect. Fixing the shared parser alone does not resolve
the form's contradictory instruction.

This review uses the 4 October export. It does not claim that either fix has
shipped or that the live desk queue has stopped changing.

## Evidence from the exported records

<IncidentAudit />

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

The table above describes historical filing in the exported support system.
Its "no ticket of its own" label is evidence of the original merge, not a claim
that the new GitHub ticket below is absent.

Read `causeOf` in `src/lib/kb/causes.ts` with the tenant directory loaded by
`locales.ts`. A day-first tenant's app/email input, or booking-v2 form input,
takes the parser path. A day-first tenant's booking-v3 form takes the hint path.
The deployed form and channel matter together. Intake tags inferred from a
reporter's browser are guesses, not root-cause evidence.

## Separate tickets and separate fixes

- [Shared month-first parser: GitHub issue 2](https://github.com/kprasad-affordablehousing/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/2). Dates written day first are read month first. Use an explicit input/date-format contract or an unambiguous representation instead of guessing slash-date order.
- [booking-v3 date hint: GitHub issue 1](https://github.com/kprasad-affordablehousing/mega-s01e03-what-it-shows/issues/1). Alder Physio, Copper Studio and Pale Harbour run a form whose hint prints `MM/DD/YYYY`, while the form reads dates in the tenant's day-first order. Make its label, example, validation and parsing agree.

The report-ID lists in `analysis.json` account for every report exactly once.
They are computed with the exported helpers, not copied from the old prose or
grouped by subject. The issues describe the evidence boundary: the export models
the failure mechanisms; it is not a production reproduction.

## Receipt time is not merge time

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

Reports start arriving in April, while the first merges appear in July.
The merge burst records desk activity on an existing backlog, not the beginning
of a new failure. Read the received and merged bars separately when deciding
how long customers have been affected.

## Do not invent a cause for the exceptions

The one-day descriptions above do not establish month/day swaps. Recover
their original input dates, tenant and recipient time zones, event payloads,
and display/parser behavior before asserting a timezone or daylight-saving
defect. Keep these reports visible in their filed group and track follow-up;
do not silently discard them or claim the parser fix is sufficient.

## Change the triage decision, not just this incident

The [triage page](/kb/triage/dedupe) and
[accepted decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) were reviewed in
June 2024. The rendered layout marks them stale. Their accepted status does not
make subject-only deduplication reliable.

The triage explorer supplies a counterexample: for Alder Physio, identical
subjects in an app report and a booking-v3 form report correspond to different
causes. Conversely, different subjects can describe the same parser path.
Missing tenant information means investigate, not automatically merge.

Propose a new decision that supersedes subject-only matching: use subjects for
search, then compare the tenant's locale, deployed form, channel and reproduced
mechanism before merging. Preserve accepted decision 0001 as history. Review
each new report against the separate tickets and refresh this page when the
live queue or deployed forms change.

